    "searchText": "sign in and recover access sign in, register, accept an invitation, verify your email address and reset a password, and understand why signing in is not the same as membership. sign in and recover access always start from the proctor address your institution gave you. don't type your sign-in details into a copied page, into an address someone sent you that you don't trust, or into a proctor desktop window. when desktop needs you to sign in, it opens your institution's sign-in page in your browser. choose where to start you already have an account open /login . the page shows only the sign-in methods that are switched on right now, both by your institution's access policy (which keeps a history of its versions) and by the sign-in providers set up on the server. - local login uses your username or email address, whichever the form accepts, and your account password. if you have mfa turned on, you enter the second factor as part of the same sign-in. - external login sends you to the provider you pick on the page. proctor recognizes your account by the provider's fixed identifier for you. it never quietly merges two accounts because a provider returned a matching email address. - if you see several providers, choose the one you first connected to your account. an external identity that isn't linked to an account doesn't become one just because its email address looks like yours. signing in creates a web session . it doesn't accept an invitation, add you to a class or give you a role. your institution sent you an invitation use the complete /join link from the most recent email. an invitation is made for one purpose, before you have an account. it may create your user or find your existing one, and in the same step it sets up exactly the relationship or set of roles it names. the page may let you set a local password, use an enabled sign-in provider, or accept while you are already signed in. whichever you choose, you get exactly the same fixed set of access. a replacement invitation cancels the old one. resending an invitation sends a fresh link, but it never widens what the invitation grants. if the link has expired, been revoked or replaced, has already been used, or is damaged, the page tells you it can't be used and nothing else happens. ask the person who invited you for a new invitation rather than trying the old link again and again. public registration is available open /register only when the page says public local registration is switched on. registering creates your user and starts email verification. it doesn't create an affiliation, a membership or a role binding. when registration is off, you need an invitation. in that case the page doesn't ask for your email address just to tell you whether an invitation exists. verify your email address the first administrator account, and any newly registered local account, starts with an email address that isn't verified yet. use the newest verification email. verifying shows only that you can read that mailbox. it doesn't prove who you legally are, your relationship with the institution, or what you are allowed to do. if the email doesn't arrive: 1. check the address in a safe place, such as your account page or an administrator's view, rather than posting it in a support channel. 2. ask for a new verification email once. 3. ask an operator whether the institution's mail delivery is working. 4. throw away older links once you have asked for a new one. reset a local password when local login is available, open /account/forgot-password and submit your account identifier once. you get the same general reply whether or not a matching account exists. if an email arrives, open the newest /account/reset-password link and choose a new password before the link expires. resetting your password changes only your password. it doesn't quietly turn off mfa or give you any academic permissions. if local login is switched off, the sign-in page hides the reset option, although the api still answers in a way that doesn't reveal whether an account exists. what sign-in error messages mean message what to do --- --- invalid credentials enter your details again with the method you chose. don't try to work out whether the account exists. mfa required or invalid enter your second factor, or use one recovery code you haven't used before. never send a recovery code to support. method disabled use another method already connected to the same account, or ask an administrator about the policy. account disabled contact your institution. creating another account won't give you back the access the original one had. session limit reached sign out an old session from your account security page, or ask an administrator for help. active attempt session lock go back to the desktop session where you are already taking the exam. proctor won't create a second user session alongside it. provider unavailable try again once the provider or the server feature is working again. don't try to link accounts by matching email addresses."
