    "description": "Look after your password, MFA, Web and Desktop Sessions, Desktop Registrations and Personal Access Tokens.",
    "href": "/account/security",
    "searchText": "secure your account look after your password, mfa, web and desktop sessions, desktop registrations and personal access tokens. secure your account your account's security is made up of several separate controls, and each does a different job: - a password or an external identity proves that you are the user. - mfa makes that proof stronger. - a session keeps one signed-in app or browser active. - a desktop registration records a public key you have chosen to trust. - a personal access token is a credential for automation. it helps to keep them apart in your head. if you think of them all as one “login token”, it is easy to miss what happens when you revoke one of them. choose and change a password proctor follows nist sp 800-63b. you don't have to mix particular kinds of character, and passwords don't expire on a schedule. length matters most. proctor refuses a new password, with a message explaining why, when it: - is shorter or longer than the limits set for your installation; - is one character repeated, a short repeated pattern such as abcabc… , a run such as abcdef… or 98765… , or a row of the keyboard such as qwerty… ; - is on a built-in list of common passwords, in any mix of upper and lower case, or is one of those passwords followed only by digits and symbols ( sunshine2026! ); - contains your username, the part of your email address before @ , or your name; - matches one of your recent passwords. your operator decides how many are remembered (five by default). the check happens on the proctor server, and your password is never sent to an outside breach-checking service. the same rules apply when your account is created, when you accept an invitation, and when you set, change or reset a password. a passphrase of several unrelated words is the easiest way to meet them. to change your password from your account, you need a session where you signed in recently and strongly. the browser you use to make the change stays signed in. when the change goes through: - every other session on your account is signed out; - any unused password-reset link stops working; - a \"password changed\" notice goes to your account's email address. undo an email change you didn't expect when the email address on your account changes, proctor emails the previous address. the message contains a single-use undo this change link, which works for a limited time after the change (72 hours by default). if you didn't expect the change, open the link. the page shows the previous address it will put back, the new address partly hidden, and when the change happened. choose undo change to go ahead. proctor then: - puts the previous address back and marks it as verified, because opening the link proved you can read that mailbox; - signs out every session on the account; - cancels any pending verification and password-reset links for the address that was replaced; - records the undo in the audit history. if another account now uses the previous address, proctor refuses the undo. contact your institution if that happens. the link never signs you in. afterwards, sign in again, and reset your password if you need to. add mfa and keep recovery codes offline setting up mfa takes two steps. first you start the setup, then you turn it on by entering a valid code. until you have finished the second step, mfa isn't protecting you. keep your recovery codes offline, and somewhere separate from your password. each recovery code works once. if you create a new set, the old set stops working. your account security page shows when you last changed your password, how many recovery codes you have left out of a full set, and how long a fresh confirmation lasts on this installation. turning off mfa and creating new recovery codes both need a session that is recent or strong enough for that action. if your session is too old, sign in again through the normal account pages. a personal access token can't be used instead. check your sign-in methods before removing one your account can have a local password and one or more external identities. you connect a provider while signed in, through browser pages made for that one purpose. proctor links the provider's fixed identifier for you. it never picks an account by matching email addresses. before you remove a password or unlink a provider, make sure you will still have another method you can use under the current access policy. proctor won't let you remove your last usable method. unlinking one external identity signs out only the sessions that were signed in through that exact identity. that includes desktop sessions you approved from one of those web sessions. your other provider identities aren't affected. review and sign out sessions the session list is a security record. it isn't a list of devices guessed from browser details. to decide whether you expect a session, look at when it was created, when it was last seen, how it signed in, what type of app it is, and whether it has been revoked. - revoke a single session when you no longer trust that browser or app. - revoke all of your sessions if you think someone has stolen your sign-in details. your current browser will be signed out too. - an administrator can revoke sessions for a user who is disabled or whose account has been taken over. that doesn't delete the user or their audit history. a revoked session stays revoked. closing a browser window is not the same as revoking the session on the server. desktop registrations are permanent records a desktop registration ties together one user, one institution and the thumbprint of one p-256 public key. the matching private key stays in the desktop app. the account page doesn't show the public jwk or the thumbprint on purpose. it shows what you need to review the registration: the device, the platform, the build, when it was created, when it was last used, and whether it has been revoked. revoking a desktop registration can't be undone. it revokes every session and credential tied to that registration. to use that device again, you need to approve it again in the browser, and desktop creates a new key. proctor doesn't offer an “undo”, and it never quietly recreates a revoked registration. keep personal access tokens limited personal access tokens are bearer credentials for automation, and they only work on actions that allow them. they are not web sessions. they can't be used for actions that need a session or a recent sign-in, and they never work for taking an exam in desktop. you can copy a token's value only once, when you create it. store it in a secret manager. revoke it when you stop using the integration, or if you think it may have been exposed. if you think your account has been compromised 1. from a browser you trust, at your institution's proctor address, revoke the affected session, desktop registration or personal access token. 2. if you aren't sure what was affected, revoke all of your sessions and change your local password, or recover the affected external provider account. if your account's email address was changed without your permission, use the undo link sent to the previous address first. 3. create new mfa recovery codes if someone may have read the ones you stored. 4. ask an administrator to look at the relevant audit events, by time and by what was affected. 5. never attach credentials, raw provider data, exam answers or large amounts of browser data to your report."
