    "description": "What the in-exam AI assistant can do for you, when it asks before acting, and why you can always read your own conversation.",
    "href": "/candidate/assistant",
    "searchText": "use the assistant what the in-exam ai assistant can do for you, when it asks before acting, and why you can always read your own conversation. use the assistant some exams offer an ai assistant in proctor desktop. if yours does, the assistant panel lets you ask it questions during your attempt. your exam manager decides how much it may help, and desktop shows you which model you are talking to. if your exam doesn't offer it, there is no panel. the server and the api support the assistant. the desktop app's code isn't in this repository, so this page describes what the assistant does without guessing at the exact labels on desktop's screens. what it can do your exam sets one help level: level what the assistant does --- --- tutor explains concepts, the language and its libraries. it can't see your files. reviewer also reads your files and, if your exam has a terminal, its screen. it explains errors and gives hints, but doesn't write your code. pair also suggests changes as a diff. nothing changes until you apply it yourself. if your exam enables execution, it can run commands and start interactive sessions. agent also changes your files and runs commands itself, as your exam allows. the assistant can only see what you can see in this attempt. it never types into your terminal: its commands run separately, and their changes are marked as the assistant's. some replies may be shortened to a hint. your exam manager can ask the assistant to keep back certain topics or long pieces of code, and proctor checks replies before you see them. it asks before it acts unless your exam says otherwise, the assistant asks you before it runs a command, starts a session, types into one, or changes a file. you see the command, the destination session and its exact text and keys, or the diff. you can accept or decline. when your exam requires approval, each new input has its own request. if you don't answer within 5 minutes, the request is declined. you can stop a reply at any time, including a command that runs as part of it. interactive sessions stay open across replies. watch or stop an interactive session an interactive session lets the assistant try a program that waits for input. you can watch its screen and stop the session. its screen is read-only. to type into the program yourself, open it in your own terminal. the assistant can have two sessions open at once. a lost connection, a suspension, an environment freeze, or submitting your attempt ends open sessions. a session's requested timeout also has to fit the time remaining in your exam's command budget. limits and outages your exam limits how much you can use the assistant, for example how many messages you can send. when a limit is reached, the assistant tells you and stops. if the assistant is unavailable, it says so. either way, your exam carries on: the assistant never stops you working or submitting. you can only have one reply in progress at a time. you can read your whole conversation everything you and the assistant say, and everything it reads, runs or changes, is recorded with your attempt. you can read your own conversation during the attempt. after submission, you can download the sealed transcript as json through get /api/v1/exam-attempts/ exam attempt id /assistant/export . the download contains the digest from your submission receipt and remains available until the transcript is retired. your exam managers read the same record when they review your submission. using the assistant in an exam that offers it isn't misconduct, and proctor raises no integrity flags for it. what leaves proctor the assistant sends your messages, and at reviewer and above the files and output it reads, to the model provider your institution chose. by default, proctor doesn't tell the model your name, email address or id. it can't remove personal details you write yourself, so keep them out of your code and your messages."
