    "href": "/candidate/browser-and-privacy",
    "searchText": "the exam browser and your privacy what the browser policy lets you open, what proctor records about the pages you visit, and what it never collects. the exam browser and your privacy when the exam's selected revision turns on browser policy, desktop gives you one exam browser. its allowlist decides which pages you can open at the top level. it can match on https address, host, start of the path, redirects, and a rule for which page you start on. it doesn't turn general web browsing into part of the exam. what can be recorded browser activity covers opening and closing the browser, top-level navigation or redirects that were allowed, and top-level navigation that was blocked. each event that is kept has: - details the server attaches, about your participation, the event's source and its sequence number; - the policy revision in force at the time; - the time on your computer; - one reason, from a fixed list; - a cut-down version of the address. how much of the address is kept depends on what happened: - for allowed https navigation, proctor may keep the host in its standard form, the port if it isn't the default one, and the path. - for blocked http, it may keep the same parts, with the scheme http . - for any other scheme that isn't allowed, it keeps only the scheme, in lower case. - for an invalid url, it keeps an empty scheme, host and path. what proctor never collects query strings, fragments, user names or passwords in urls, page content, page titles, referrers, headers, cookies, page structure (dom data), downloads, local file paths and anything carried inside a scheme are not part of the policy and are not recorded. the first version of integrity evidence also leaves out screenshots, webcam, clipboard, source code and any other open-ended data. activity is evidence kept to a minimum to protect your privacy. it isn't a verdict. neither the time on your computer nor anything desktop claims decides how serious something is, or whether you did anything wrong. if records are missing or have gaps, proctor marks that uncertainty openly. the server never invents navigation that didn't happen. desktop may keep sending records while the sitting is paused, or while a correction is waiting for you to acknowledge it. that lets the records catch up, but it doesn't let you interact with the exam. who can see it and how long it is kept your ordinary browser history is visible only to the exam's appointed managers who also have the browser activity permission. a general administrator override doesn't give anyone access to this history. some blocked navigation may also create a separate, cut-down copy as integrity evidence, for authorized review. the server gives you a current collection notice. it shows your institution's retention period for browser records, and whether the policy may create integrity evidence. a retention period of zero days means no expiry has been set. the retention clock depends on the records for the closed sitting being complete in a way that counts as eligible. preservation holds and incomplete records can mean records are kept longer. integrity evidence has its own retention period, so your ordinary history may expire while a copy kept for review remains. the notice tells you what is collected. it isn't asking you to grant a new permission or give consent."
