    "description": "Get Proctor Desktop signed in with a registered key, then learn what Proctor sets up for you when you enter, including your Attempt, Participation, Connection and settings.",
    "href": "/candidate/prepare-and-enter",
    "searchText": "prepare and enter an attempt get proctor desktop signed in with a registered key, then learn what proctor sets up for you when you enter, including your attempt, participation, connection and settings. prepare and enter an attempt check the server and your desktop build start from the proctor address your institution gave you. desktop's discovery step locks onto that address. then system/ping checks your exact desktop release, build, platform, architecture and realtime protocol. stop if the server reports maintenance, update required, incompatible server, unsupported target or revoked build. don't edit the values desktop reports to make it look compatible. if desktop doesn't have an active session tied to its registered key, go through desktop authorization first. the private key stays inside desktop, and every protected request carries a fresh dpop proof. use candidate exam activity candidate exam activity is the list that tells you which exams you can open and what you can do with each one, and the server is in charge of it. only choose an action the server is currently showing you. a sitting can appear there before you have an attempt. proctor creates your one ready attempt the first time you are allowed to enter. if you never enter, proctor doesn't create an attempt for you. what proctor checks when you enter each time you enter, proctor checks again that: - you are currently a member of the exact class; - the sitting is in the right state, and its current revision is selected; - desktop has a compatible registered key and an active desktop session; - the session lock that allows only one unresolved attempt per user is respected; - desktop's runtime state is acceptable, and it supports your settings; - the attempt is ready, rather than finished or blocked. the first time you are let in, proctor starts one active participation generation. it also creates one connection, tied to that exact desktop session and its registered key. a continuity credential proves that this generation is yours. it must only ever be held in desktop's protected memory and in sensitive request headers. it must never appear in urls, logs, support messages or browser storage. your attempt settings are fixed when you enter when you are let in, proctor fixes one attempt configuration for the rest of your attempt. it is built from your personal settings and from the list of settings your desktop build supports. it includes only approved preferences that both sides understand, for accessibility, appearance, the editor, motion, announcements, the cursor and keyboard commands. hidden settings and the versions of the settings list are kept only as a record of where the configuration came from. reconnecting, or being allowed back in, doesn't rebuild it. if desktop can't support the configuration's list of settings, proctor leaves your attempt ready rather than quietly dropping your preferences."
