    "id": "operation:connectCandidateTerminal",
    "kind": "endpoint",
    "group": "Attempts and workspaces",
    "title": "Upgrade to a terminal's binary socket",
    "description": "Upgrades to a binary WebSocket attached to one terminal. Any Proctor node serves it by reaching the execution machine itself, so a reconnect may land on another node and resume exactly. The terminal is attached before the upgrade: a refusal answers as a problem. Every message is one binary frame whose first byte is its type; integers are big-endian. The client's first message is ATTACH or SCREEN. Client frames: - `0x01` ATTACH `offset:u64`: stream output from offset. A reconnecting client sends the output offset it holds, and output still in the terminal's history is replayed, so full-screen programs come back intact. - `0x02` SCREEN: answer with the screen. As the first message, also stream output from the screen's offset; a client with no screen of its own draws it, then continues. - `0x03` INPUT `offset:u64` `data` (1 to 16,384 bytes): input written at offset. Bytes the terminal already took are dropped, so after a lost connection the client resends everything after the last INPUT offset it saw, and nothing types twice. - `0x04` RESIZE `cols:u16` `rows:u16`: the terminal's new window, 1 to 1,000 each way. Server frames: - `0x81` OUTPUT `offset:u64` `data`: output bytes at offset, at most 32 KiB. - `0x82` TRUNCATED `from:u64` `to:u64`: output from to to that the history no longer holds. - `0x83` EXIT `code:i32` `signal:i32`: the terminal exited; the socket then closes normally. - `0x84` INPUT `offset:u64`: the terminal's input offset after an INPUT, accepted or not. - `0x85` SCREEN `offset:u64` `cols:u16` `rows:u16` `cursor_row:u16` `cursor_col:u16` `flags:u8` `data`: the screen as of offset; flag bit 0 is the alternate screen, bit 1 a hidden cursor, and data redraws it on a blank terminal of that size. - `0x86` ERROR `code`: a refused frame, by its public error code in UTF-8, such as exam.attempt.invalid for input past the terminal's input offset; the socket stays open. Several sockets may read one terminal, such as the candidate's two windows; they share one input offset. The socket closes normally after EXIT or a malformed frame, with 4003 once the Attempt is sealed, its Participation ends, or its machine ends or is replaced, and with 1011 on a server failure. Access is checked again every 15 seconds while the socket is open. Terminal bytes never travel between Proctor nodes, and output is never recorded.",
